WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:05.950
We have to understand that every rule that
gets written has a cost.

00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:11.910
I don't think parents get that under the headline
'We're gonna keep your children safe.

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:13.559
We're gonna protect the kids.'

00:00:13.559 --> 00:00:17.499
that that's really code for 'We're taking
your rights away.'

00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:26.830
Hi I'm Nick Gillespie with Reason TV and today
we're talking with Abby Schachter, she is

00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:30.570
a long-time journalist and the author of the
fabulous new book

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:34.760
'No Child Left Alone: Getting the Government out of Parenting.'

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:36.210
Abby, thanks for talking to us.

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:37.210
It's great to be here.

00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:41.949
The book is all about how government at all
levels and other kind of butt-insky groups

00:00:41.949 --> 00:00:46.999
are getting more and more involved in the
raising of children, of child-rearing.

00:00:46.999 --> 00:00:52.399
Give some of the most exemplary illustrations
of just how annoying and frustrating this

00:00:52.399 --> 00:00:56.599
is, and why don't you start with your daycare
experience that you start the book with.

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:57.100
Right.

00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:02.760
So I didn't set out to be a radical, I just
wanted my kids swaddled at day care.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:08.120
But that isn't okay with the state of Pennsylvania,
and it's where i drew the line

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:10.240
and got into this subject.

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:15.189
We have four children, they've all gone to
state licensed day care, there were a huge

00:01:15.189 --> 00:01:21.770
number of rules and regulations that were
annoying but we- I lived with them.

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:26.840
And then the fourth one was born and we brought
him to day care at nine weeks of age and they

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:29.260
said 'What do you want us to know about him?'
and I said 'One thing:

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:30.780
please swaddle him, tightly.'

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:33.740
And that means wrapping the baby in a kind
of blanket.

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:34.979
So that they sleep easier.

00:01:34.979 --> 00:01:36.259
Like baby Jesus, right?

00:01:36.259 --> 00:01:37.729
It's been done for thousands of years.

00:01:37.729 --> 00:01:42.450
It was the only parenting book we actually
read and followed: doctor Harvey Karp, I'm

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:45.689
a big proselytizer for him, happiest baby
on the block.

00:01:45.689 --> 00:01:48.630
All our children sleep because of swaddling.

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:50.159
So that was it, that's all I wanted.

00:01:50.159 --> 00:01:54.399
And the day care said 'We need a doctor's
note because the state of Pennsylvania otherwise

00:01:54.399 --> 00:01:57.100
bans swaddling in day care.'

00:01:57.100 --> 00:02:00.780
So I said 'That is crazy, I've never heard
of such a thing.'

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:01.280
and I-

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:02.800
They pushed you too far.

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:09.039
They pushed me too far, I just said 'That
is it' and I said 'I'm donning my cape.

00:02:09.039 --> 00:02:12.360
I am now captain mommy, I'm taking charge.'

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:15.070
And I thought 'oh okay I'll just get the doctor's
note.'

00:02:15.070 --> 00:02:19.340
I actually started off thinking this will
be a reasonable, reasonably easy thing to

00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:21.879
get done and it turns out it's not.

00:02:21.879 --> 00:02:29.560
Because some of the same people who are pushing
this kind of overly broad definitions of safety

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:37.990
and lack of acceptance of risk are the people
who- the doctors, pediatricians, go to to

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:41.819
get opinions for how they should advise their
patients.

00:02:41.819 --> 00:02:47.150
So that is, I mean, one clear case of government
regulation or, you know, some kind of authority

00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:48.150
coming in and saying 'No.

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:51.470
You gotta do it this way in this circumstance.'

00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:52.640
What are a couple of others?

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:59.159
So the worst example are the dozens of cases
of the state coming and taking custody of

00:02:59.159 --> 00:03:01.930
children who are morbidly obese.

00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:03.310
These are heartbreaking stories.

00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:10.340
The family tends to be unhealthy, not just
the child and they are really unhealthy but

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:18.049
the state says that the best they can do is
remove a child from it's family, causing other

00:03:18.049 --> 00:03:22.950
damage, but they're gonna try and 'solve'
in quotes this one problem-

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:25.400
And that's the super fat kid?

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:26.610
That's the super fat kid.

00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:27.610
Does it work?

00:03:27.610 --> 00:03:28.720
Is it effective at least?

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:30.560
Well, it depends who you talk to.

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:33.900
There are lots of doctors who say 'just force
them to have surgery.'

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:39.300
I mean, bariatric surgery, a teenager could
live a much longer life then

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:40.900
trying to change behavior.

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:45.220
Behavior modification is actually very difficult
to achieve successfully and yet the state

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:47.409
is basically saying 'That's what we're gonna
do.'

00:03:47.409 --> 00:03:51.730
Yeah, I was gonna say, I mean, that's hard
for individuals, you know, who are really

00:03:51.730 --> 00:03:57.110
dedicated to but then to get a bureaucrat
to do, to make somebody else pay attention.

00:03:57.110 --> 00:04:00.800
You also talk about the war on fun and the
kind of play deficit.

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:05.599
Right, so there's- there are all kinds of
experts who will talk about, Peter Gray talks

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:10.920
about this a lot, you know, the deficit of
unsupervised, imaginative,

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:12.560
free play amongst kids.

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:15.060
Some of that is parents, they don't wanna
let the kids out of the house

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:16.920
so they over-schedule them, okay.

00:04:16.930 --> 00:04:21.650
But if you don't have recess at school, which
a lot of parents are fighting for, if you

00:04:21.650 --> 00:04:28.509
ban certain activities, sledding in a public
playground, running in a public space, pushing

00:04:28.509 --> 00:04:30.190
other kids on the swing.

00:04:30.190 --> 00:04:34.620
So all kinds of activities end up being banned,
not because the government comes along or

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:38.210
the school system comes along and says 'hey
we don't want these kids to have any fun.'

00:04:38.210 --> 00:04:45.840
But they are defining a problem and they are
solving it in their easiest, sort of, overly

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:48.200
broad way which is: we're just gonna ban it.

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:51.020
So why is this kinda stuff happening now?

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:57.080
I've been writing about the different experience
of childhood, you know, from my childhood

00:04:57.080 --> 00:04:59.330
to my kid's childhood for twenty years.

00:04:59.330 --> 00:05:03.580
My kids are now 22 and 14 and in the 90s when
i started thinking about this stuff I realized,

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:09.890
wow their lives are so different than the
one I grew up in and mine was on another planet

00:05:09.890 --> 00:05:11.229
from my parents.

00:05:11.229 --> 00:05:13.110
So why is this kind of structure happening?

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:18.650
Is it simply that in two income households
both parents are away and you put your kids

00:05:18.650 --> 00:05:21.160
into institutionalized settings more?

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:27.410
Is it the ridiculous over-estimations of risk,
what's going on here?

00:05:27.410 --> 00:05:29.270
I think that it's a combination of things.

00:05:29.270 --> 00:05:34.990
I think there is been several media hysterias
having to do with children.

00:05:34.990 --> 00:05:35.990
Right?

00:05:35.990 --> 00:05:42.949
Abduction was one, and this is a mythology
that has so taken root that we have had low

00:05:42.949 --> 00:05:49.769
crime rates for 15 years and I still get people
reacting to my book saying

00:05:49.769 --> 00:05:51.209
'But the kid could get abducted.'

00:05:51.209 --> 00:05:51.729
you know?

00:05:51.729 --> 00:05:54.169
Why does that linger so long? and how does
it

00:05:54.169 --> 00:05:58.039
continue to inform policy and kind of mental maps?

00:05:58.039 --> 00:06:04.349
Well, because they went- we moved from abductions,
let's say, to the hysteria over satanism at

00:06:04.349 --> 00:06:05.819
day care, right?

00:06:05.819 --> 00:06:06.819
That was the 90s.

00:06:06.819 --> 00:06:09.200
I would have paid extra.

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:11.560
If I coulda gotten my kid into the satanist
day care.

00:06:11.560 --> 00:06:15.770
And at least if I could have done that now,
it would have been cheaper.

00:06:15.770 --> 00:06:18.470
There would have been swaddling, I'm sure.

00:06:18.470 --> 00:06:19.620
Satanists would swaddle!

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:22.699
I, that would have been a reason to try it
out.

00:06:22.699 --> 00:06:31.440
I think that it's all these hysterias that
have taken hold and anxiety is really hard

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:32.440
to combat.

00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:35.050
I mean that's really, I think that's a large
part of this.

00:06:35.050 --> 00:06:39.990
You also talk about, you reference Philip
K Howard, the author whose best known book

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:42.729
is 'The Death of Common Sense.'

00:06:42.729 --> 00:06:48.580
And he talks a lot about how people, oftentimes
in a good natured way, or a good faith way

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:53.330
say 'Look this is just common sense, let's
put a law or a process.'

00:06:53.330 --> 00:06:56.319
and then it goes in a very different direction.

00:06:56.319 --> 00:06:57.761
Is that a contributing factor here?

00:06:57.761 --> 00:07:00.150
The kind of over-lawyering of everyday life.

00:07:00.150 --> 00:07:04.660
The over-lawyering, I mean, look: part of
it is practical, the trial lawyers get work,

00:07:04.660 --> 00:07:05.660
right?

00:07:05.660 --> 00:07:12.979
If you can sue and we don't have a reform
as simple as loser pays then you have an incentive

00:07:12.979 --> 00:07:16.740
to sue the municipality if your kid gets hurt
sledding.

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:21.390
And these municipalities are struggling to
pay their pensions so they don't wanna fight

00:07:21.390 --> 00:07:22.390
a lawsuit.

00:07:22.390 --> 00:07:25.479
They'll settle, and then they'll just ban
the activity.

00:07:25.479 --> 00:07:31.590
So some of these critiques and in Howard's
case he's absolutely right also about the

00:07:31.590 --> 00:07:33.310
nature of the regulation.

00:07:33.310 --> 00:07:41.780
We don't say out value is X and therefore
we want to allow people to achieve that value

00:07:41.780 --> 00:07:46.639
whether it be safety or reducing risk, you
find a way to do it.

00:07:46.639 --> 00:07:52.750
What we say is, you have to have the hooks
in day care this far apart because that's

00:07:52.750 --> 00:07:54.320
the safety standard, right?

00:07:54.320 --> 00:07:55.320
So-

00:07:55.320 --> 00:08:03.470
So we're not really being led by general principles
or goals it's rather being very kind of literal

00:08:03.470 --> 00:08:04.580
and specific.

00:08:04.580 --> 00:08:08.509
Absolutely and you see that everywhere.

00:08:08.509 --> 00:08:14.889
And you see that with, sort of, the Consumer
Product Safety Commission, their labels, right?

00:08:14.889 --> 00:08:18.539
They are also- There's no common sense in
their warning labels.

00:08:18.539 --> 00:08:22.970
Because, what do they put on the warning labels
for baby seats?

00:08:22.970 --> 00:08:27.060
Don't put this on a high surface where the
baby might fall.

00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:30.410
I mean you would think this is common sense
right?

00:08:30.410 --> 00:08:34.450
Instead they hounded the manufacturer of a
particular- of the Bumbo.

00:08:34.450 --> 00:08:36.570
And now they changed the name of the product.

00:08:36.570 --> 00:08:40.130
So now it's called the Bumbo floor seat right?

00:08:40.130 --> 00:08:45.110
As if it was too difficult for people to understand
that it was not meant to be put up on a table

00:08:45.110 --> 00:08:49.221
or a chair it was meant to sit on the floor
and that would be the safest place for a baby.

00:08:49.221 --> 00:08:55.000
Well here's a challenge, is it possible, you
know, we know from looking at the results

00:08:55.000 --> 00:09:00.180
of school tests for K through 12 students,
for, you know, going back to the early 70s

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:03.650
when we started doing this, we're not- the
kids aren't getting any smarter maybe parents

00:09:03.650 --> 00:09:06.400
aren't as smart as they once were.

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:07.400
That's also possible.

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:11.860
I mean, I wouldn't put it- but I would say
that I have found some very smart parents

00:09:11.860 --> 00:09:13.110
writing this book.

00:09:13.110 --> 00:09:19.021
No Child Left Alone I tried to enter as many
examples of parents who, like myself, just

00:09:19.021 --> 00:09:23.020
said 'This is the stupidest thing I've ever
heard of in my life and I wanna be left alone

00:09:23.020 --> 00:09:24.390
to parent my own kid.'

00:09:24.390 --> 00:09:26.630
And talk a bit about, so what is the cure?

00:09:26.630 --> 00:09:27.890
Where do we go from here?

00:09:27.890 --> 00:09:32.540
Because you're against the idea that there
is a kind of big switch where we can go from

00:09:32.540 --> 00:09:35.020
insanity to sanity.

00:09:35.020 --> 00:09:40.330
What are the types of activities, what are
the types of things that can happen to kind

00:09:40.330 --> 00:09:45.830
of free our children and by extension free
us as parents to, you know, kind of live in

00:09:45.830 --> 00:09:48.470
a world that is more interesting and exciting

00:09:48.470 --> 00:09:51.340
and ultimately better for both parents and children?

00:09:51.340 --> 00:09:58.060
So the two things that I would suggest are
one: bring the problem solving closer to the

00:09:58.060 --> 00:09:59.100
supposed problem.

00:09:59.100 --> 00:10:04.500
I had to go and find the people who made up
this rule about swaddling and they weren't

00:10:04.500 --> 00:10:08.500
in my state and they weren't even accountable.

00:10:08.500 --> 00:10:13.790
I couldn't yell at them because it wasn't
their fault they wrote a nice book of guidelines.

00:10:13.790 --> 00:10:20.240
And the other thing I would say is that we
have to understand that every rule that gets

00:10:20.240 --> 00:10:21.600
written has a cost.

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:27.650
I don't think parents get that under the headline
'We're gonna keep your children safe.

00:10:27.650 --> 00:10:29.610
We're gonna protect the kids.'

00:10:29.610 --> 00:10:32.850
that that's really code for 'We're taking
your rights away.'

00:10:32.850 --> 00:10:37.360
Would you draw a connection between the way
that we treat our children when they're kind

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:43.130
of in, you know, K through 12 or when they're
living at home with us, would you trace a

00:10:43.130 --> 00:10:48.850
line from that and kind of the infantilization
of kids in a way that is more than it used

00:10:48.850 --> 00:10:50.040
to be and parents.

00:10:50.040 --> 00:10:51.890
Because parents aren't allowed to make choices.

00:10:51.890 --> 00:10:56.640
Is that part of what we're seeing on college
campuses when we see either administrators

00:10:56.640 --> 00:11:03.210
or students themselves demanding a kind of
more infantile safe space in college campuses.

00:11:03.210 --> 00:11:05.820
I mean it's hard for me to say that it's a
direct line.

00:11:05.820 --> 00:11:10.680
But certainly there's an influence because
what you're hearing about what happens to

00:11:10.680 --> 00:11:14.520
college kids is they get there and they don't
know how to

00:11:14.520 --> 00:11:17.880
live in a world that isn't a safe space.

00:11:17.880 --> 00:11:21.320
So they're demanding it because that's what
they've experienced.

00:11:21.320 --> 00:11:27.790
If you're also coming from a place where you
were never, you never rode the city bus anywhere,

00:11:27.790 --> 00:11:33.160
you never got anywhere on your own or your
time was all managed by somebody else, you

00:11:33.160 --> 00:11:38.650
really aren't gonna have that many skills
and you really are going to think- who would've

00:11:38.650 --> 00:11:43.120
in your life come to you and said 'No, you
actually have to do this for yourself now'

00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:48.550
because some parents haven't been doing that
and certainly you're teachers haven't been

00:11:48.550 --> 00:11:54.120
saying that and the government is not saying
that, they've been saying the opposite.

00:11:54.120 --> 00:11:55.350
Well we will leave it there.

00:11:55.350 --> 00:11:59.880
We have been talking with Abby Schachter,
she is the author of the excellent book

00:11:59.880 --> 00:12:02.990
No Child Left Alone: Getting the Government out
of Parenting.

00:12:02.990 --> 00:12:04.230
Abby thanks for talking to us.

00:12:04.230 --> 00:12:05.430
Pleasure, thank you.

00:12:05.430 --> 00:12:07.390
For Reason TV, I'm Nick Gillespie.

